artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an infrared tip-tilt wavefront sensor that observes a two-dimensional image I(x,y) of an astronomical target on a pixelated detector. The image is modeled as

  I(x,y) = (O * P)(x - dx, y - dy) + n(x,y),

where O(x,y) is the intrinsic (unknown) intensity distribution of an extended source (i.e., not well approximated by a point), P(x,y) is a (possibly partially-corrected, possibly time-varying) point spread function, * denotes two-dimensional convolution, (dx,dy) is an unknown image displacement to be estimated at each short exposure, and n(x,y) is detector and photon noise. The target is faint, so the total signal-to-noise ratio per frame is low, and the support of O is comparable to or larger than the diffraction core of P.

Design and analyze an estimator (dx̂, dŷ) of the displacement (dx, dy) from a single short-exposure frame I that:

(a) does not assume O is a point source (so simple intensity-weighted centroids, matched-filter centroids tuned to a point PSF, quad-cell centroids, and correlation against a point template are not adequate);
(b) is robust to the unknown or only partially known shape of O and to slow changes in P;
(c) attains, or approaches, the Cramér–Rao lower bound for displacement estimation under realistic noise (mixed Poisson photon noise and Gaussian read noise);
(d) is computationally feasible at kilohertz update rates suitable for real-time adaptive-optics tip-tilt control.

Specifically: determine whether such an estimator exists, give an explicit construction (e.g., an iteratively updated reference template, a maximum-likelihood or Bayesian formulation, or a learned estimator), and prove bounds on its bias and variance as a function of the source flux, the morphology of O, and the statistics of P.

